### =============================================
### CIRCUIT BREAKER TESTING WORKFLOW
### =============================================
### IMPORTANT: These tests are most effective when run DURING active chaos scenarios
### 1. Start chaos scenarios from chaos-demo.http first
### 2. Then run these orders to see how circuit breakers protect the system
### 3. Monitor circuit-breakers endpoint to see state changes in real-time
### =============================================

### Create order to test retry logic during service failures
### PURPOSE: This order will trigger retries if dependent services are failing
### MONITORING: Watch for retry attempts in order-processor logs
### BEST USED: During active chaos scenarios (inventory/payment failures)
POST http://localhost:3006/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customer": "circuit_breaker_test_user",
  "item": "pear",
  "total": 299.99
}

### Create high-value order to test approval workflow under stress
### PURPOSE: Tests external event handling when payment services are degraded
### MONITORING: Watch for approval workflow delays/failures during chaos
### BEST USED: During payment service chaos scenarios
POST http://localhost:3006/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customer": "approval_stress_test_user",
  "item": "premium_stress_test_widget",
  "total": 1500.00
}

### Check circuit breaker status after submitting orders
### PURPOSE: See if circuit breakers have opened due to failures
### EXPECTED DURING CHAOS: Some services may show state="open" or "half-open"
GET http://localhost:3006/circuit-breakers

### =============================================
### CIRCUIT BREAKER DEMONSTRATION GUIDE
### =============================================
### To properly demonstrate circuit breaker functionality:
###
### 1. BASELINE: Run GET /circuit-breakers (should show all closed)
### 2. START CHAOS: Execute scenarios from chaos-demo.http
### 3. STRESS TEST: Submit orders above while chaos is active
### 4. MONITOR: Watch circuit-breakers endpoint for state changes
### 5. OBSERVE: Note how circuit breakers protect against cascade failures
###
### Circuit Breaker States:
### - CLOSED: Normal operation, requests pass through
### - OPEN: Too many failures, requests fail fast (no retry)
### - HALF-OPEN: Testing if service has recovered
###
### Expected Behavior During Chaos:
### - Failure count increases as services fail
### - State transitions from closed → open after threshold
### - Fast failures protect system from cascade effects
### =============================================


